.TH NEXTAFTER 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
nextafter, nextafterf, nextafterl, nexttoward, nexttowardf, nexttowardl, nextup, nextupf, nextupl, nextdown, nextdownf, nextdownl \- next representable floating-point value
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double nextafter(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float nextafterf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double nextafterl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.PP
.BI "double nexttoward(double " x ", long double " y );
.br
.BI "float nexttowardf(float " x ", long double " y );
.br
.BI "long double nexttowardl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.PP
.BI "double nextup(double " x );
.br
.BI "float nextupf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double nextupl(long double " x );
.PP
.BI "double nextdown(double " x );
.br
.BI "float nextdownf(float " x );
.br
.BI "long double nextdownl(long double " x );
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR nextafter ()
family returns the next representable floating-point value after
.I x
in the direction of
.IR y .
If
.I x
equals
.IR y ,
the functions return
.I y
converted to the return type (so that, for example,
.BR nextafter ()
of two equal arguments returns the second argument unchanged in sign,
including the case of signed zeros).
.PP
The
.BR nexttoward ()
family behaves identically to
.BR nextafter (),
except that the second argument
.I y
is always of type
.BR "long double" .
This permits a finer-grained direction argument when the first
operand is
.B float
or
.BR double ,
and avoids loss of precision when comparing
.I x
against an extended-precision target value.
.PP
The
.BR nextup ()
family (introduced in C23 / ISO/IEC 60559:2020) returns the least
.B float
(respectively
.B double
or
.BR "long double" )
that compares greater than
.IR x ;
that is, the next representable value in the direction of positive
infinity.  The
.BR nextdown ()
family returns the greatest representable value that compares less
than
.IR x ;
that is, the next representable value in the direction of negative
infinity.  These are equivalent to
.BI nextafter( x ", +INFINITY)"
and
.BI nextafter( x ", \-INFINITY)"
respectively, except for the handling of NaN (see RETURN VALUE).
.PP
On a binary IEC 60559 platform such as Substrate, "next representable"
means a one-unit-in-the-last-place
.RB ( ULP )
step in the bit pattern: for normalized numbers the result differs from
.I x
by 2^(E\-p+1), where
.I E
is the unbiased exponent of
.I x
and
.I p
is the precision of the format (24 for
.BR float ,
53 for
.BR double ,
64 for IEEE\ 754 80-bit
.BR "long double" ).
For subnormal numbers the step is the smallest representable positive
value of the format (DBL_MIN \(mu 2^(\-52) for
.BR double ).
.PP
Crossing zero is well defined: the next value above
.B \-0.0
is the smallest positive subnormal, and the next value below
.B +0.0
is the smallest negative subnormal (in magnitude).  Stepping outward
from
.B \(+-DBL_MAX
yields
.BR \(+-INFINITY ;
stepping inward from
.B \(+-INFINITY
yields
.BR \(+-DBL_MAX .
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR nextafter ()
and
.BR nexttoward ()
return the next representable value after
.I x
in the direction of
.IR y .
If
.I x
equals
.IR y ,
.I y
(converted to the return type) is returned.
.PP
.BR nextup ()
returns the next representable value strictly greater than
.IR x ;
.BR nextdown ()
returns the next representable value strictly less than
.IR x .
.PP
The following special cases apply:
.TP
.IR x " or " y " is a NaN"
For
.BR nextafter ()
and
.BR nexttoward (),
a NaN is returned.
.TP
.B "nextup(NaN)"
returns a NaN; likewise for
.BR nextdown ().
.TP
.B "nextup(\-Inf)"
returns
.BR \-DBL_MAX
(or the format equivalent: the most negative finite value).
.TP
.B "nextup(+Inf)"
returns
.BR +Inf .
.TP
.B "nextdown(+Inf)"
returns
.B +DBL_MAX
(or the format equivalent: the largest finite value).
.TP
.B "nextdown(\-Inf)"
returns
.BR \-Inf .
.TP
.B "nextup(\-0.0)"
returns the smallest positive subnormal value of the format.
.TP
.B "nextdown(+0.0)"
returns the negative of the smallest positive subnormal value.
.PP
If the magnitude of
.I x
equals the largest finite value of the format and the result would
step outward (away from zero), a range (overflow) error occurs and the
function returns
.B \(+-HUGE_VAL
(or
.B \(+-HUGE_VALF
or
.BR \(+-HUGE_VALL )
with the sign of
.IR x .
.PP
If
.I x
is
.B \(+-0
and the result steps outward (toward
.IR y ),
or if
.I x
is the smallest positive (largest negative) subnormal stepping toward
zero, a range (underflow) error occurs and the function returns the
smallest representable subnormal value (or signed zero) with the
appropriate sign.
.SH ERRORS
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.PP
The following errors can occur:
.TP
Range error: result overflow
.I x
is finite and the result is infinite (the function steps outward from
.BR \(+-DBL_MAX ).
An overflow floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_OVERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.TP
Range error: result underflow
.I x
is finite and the result is subnormal (the function steps outward from
.B \(+-0
into the subnormal range, or between subnormals).  An underflow
floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_UNDERFLOW )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR ERANGE .
.PP
No domain error is raised when
.I x
or
.I y
is NaN; the NaN is propagated as the result.
.PP
.BR nextup ()
and
.BR nextdown ()
follow the same range-error rules as
.BR nextafter ()
when stepping past
.B \(+-DBL_MAX
or producing a subnormal result.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR nextafter (),
.BR nextafterf (),
.BR nextafterl (),
.BR nexttoward (),
.BR nexttowardf (),
.BR nexttowardl (),
.BR nextup (),
.BR nextupf (),
.BR nextupl (),
.BR nextdown (),
.BR nextdownf (),
.BR nextdownl ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
.BR nextafter (),
.BR nexttoward (),
and their
.BR f / l
variants conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.BR nextup ()
and
.BR nextdown ()
were standardized in ISO/IEC 60559:2020 and added to C in C23.  The
handling of zero, infinity, and NaN follows C99 Annex F.10.8 and the
IEC 60559 nextUp/nextDown operations.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program walks a few ULPs above
.B 1.0
to illustrate the granularity of
.B double
precision near unity, and contrasts
.BR nextup ()
with
.BR nextdown ()
at the same point:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <float.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    double x = 1.0;
    double up, down;
    int i;

    /* Step four ULPs above 1.0. */
    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        double next = nextafter(x, INFINITY);
        printf("ulp #%d above 1.0: %.17g (delta = %.3e)\en",
               i + 1, next, next - x);
        x = next;
    }

    /* nextup vs nextdown at 1.0. */
    up   = nextup(1.0);
    down = nextdown(1.0);
    printf("nextup  (1.0) = %.17g (delta = %.3e)\en",
           up,   up   - 1.0);
    printf("nextdown(1.0) = %.17g (delta = %.3e)\en",
           down, down - 1.0);

    /* Crossing zero. */
    printf("nextafter(+0, -1) = %.3e\en", nextafter(0.0, -1.0));
    printf("nextafter(-0, +1) = %.3e\en", nextafter(-0.0, 1.0));

    /* Outward overshoot from DBL_MAX. */
    printf("nextafter(DBL_MAX, INF) = %g\en",
           nextafter(DBL_MAX, INFINITY));

    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A representative output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ulp #1 above 1.0: 1.0000000000000002 (delta = 2.220e-16)
ulp #2 above 1.0: 1.0000000000000004 (delta = 2.220e-16)
ulp #3 above 1.0: 1.0000000000000007 (delta = 2.220e-16)
ulp #4 above 1.0: 1.0000000000000009 (delta = 2.220e-16)
nextup  (1.0) = 1.0000000000000002 (delta = 2.220e-16)
nextdown(1.0) = 0.99999999999999989 (delta = -1.110e-16)
nextafter(+0, -1) = -4.941e-324
nextafter(-0, +1) = 4.941e-324
nextafter(DBL_MAX, INF) = inf
.EE
.RE
.PP
Note that the step below
.B 1.0
is half the step above it: the binade
.BR [0.5,\ 1.0)
has exponent \-1, so its ULP is
.B 2^\-53
rather than
.BR 2^\-52 .
This asymmetry is intrinsic to floating-point representation, not a
quirk of the implementation.
